    <h2>FAQ</h2>

    <h3>Is Canva better than Figma?</h3>
    <p>Canva is better for marketers, educators, and non-designers who need quick, template-based visuals for social media, presentations, and print. Figma is better for professional UI and UX designers who need vector editing, prototyping, and real-time collaboration on product interfaces. For marketing content, Canva wins. For product design, Figma wins.</p>

    <h3>Which is cheaper, Canva or Figma?</h3>
    <p>Canva Pro costs $12.99 per user per month or $119.99 per year, while Figma Professional costs $12 per editor per month. Canva also offers a generous free tier that covers most marketing needs, while Figma's free tier is limited to 3 projects. For individuals and small teams that need templates, Canva offers more value. For professional design teams, Figma is slightly cheaper per editor.</p>

    <h3>Can Canva replace Figma for UI design?</h3>
    <p>No, Canva cannot replace Figma for serious UI design. Canva lacks vector editing, component variants, auto layout, interactive prototyping, and developer handoff features that professional product designers need. Canva is built for marketing and social content, not for designing app interfaces or multi-screen product flows. For UI design, Figma is the clear choice.</p>

    <h3>Can Figma replace Canva for marketing content?</h3>
    <p>Figma can create marketing visuals, but it is not a good replacement for Canva. Figma has no built-in stock photo library, no ready-made social media templates, no one-click brand kit application, and no print fulfillment. Canva is designed for fast, template-driven content creation that non-designers can use. For marketing teams, Canva is the better tool.</p>

    <h3>Do Canva and Figma work together?</h3>
    <p>Yes, and many teams use both. A common workflow is to design product interfaces and brand assets in Figma, then export them to Canva as brand templates for the marketing team to use. Canva also supports importing SVG files, so Figma vector graphics can be brought into Canva. The combined cost is around $25 per user per month, which is reasonable for teams that need both product design and marketing content.</p>

    <h3>Which is better for beginners, Canva or Figma?</h3>
    <p>Canva is significantly better for beginners. Its drag-and-drop interface, pre-made templates, and guided design tools let anyone create professional-looking visuals in minutes without design training. Figma has a steeper learning curve because it assumes knowledge of vector editing, frames, components, and prototyping. For non-designers and beginners, Canva is the clear choice.</p>

